    card title "How much richer is the richest country?"

A: Take the richest country in the world, by income per person, and the poorest. How many times richer is the richest?
    draw fl_sg n_sg

B: Ten times? Twenty, tops. Things are much cheaper in poor countries, after all.
    draw fl_bi n_bi

    ask "Set the counter to your guess." estimate.min 1 estimate.max 1000 estimate.value 130 estimate.unit times estimate.log true store r default "20"
        right: Close. The gap is about a hundred and thirty times.
        wrong: Not quite. It's about a hundred and thirty times.

    erase estimate_1

A: Here is Burundi, the poorest: about a thousand dollars per person a year. One coin.
    draw cb key

A: And Singapore: about a hundred and forty thousand. A hundred and thirty coins.
    draw rich v_sg

B: But I said things are cheaper there!
    draw day

A: They are, and it's already counted: these dollars are adjusted for prices. Burundi still lives on under three dollars a day.
    highlight key

    erase v_sg

B: Was it always this unequal?
    draw x130

    erase key day

A: No. In 1820, among the countries with estimates, the richest, Britain, was only about five times richer than the poorest, Nepal.
    draw fl_uk n_uk fl_np n_np ukc cn y1820

A: Back then nearly everyone was poor. Growth took off in a few places, and by these long-run estimates the poorest countries today are no richer than Nepal was then.
    highlight cn cb

B: Five coins to a hundred and thirty. Next time someone tells me the world was always this way, I'm bringing my coins.
    highlight rich ukc

